# Evidence: Command-free Linux code proof exposed an underspecified campaign

Status: verified
Type: evidence
Updated: 2026-08-26
Campaign: prove-command-free-first-pass-code-test-campaign-on-linux

## Result

Campaign 078 did not satisfy the code/test proof gate. It must not be replayed.

The first preparation attempt stopped before capsule persistence because generic relevance selection
ranked routing documentation ahead of implementation source. The shared selector was repaired to
expand hyphenated focus terms, exclude `work/` lifecycle artifacts, prioritize relevant source
excerpts ahead of preferred routing documents, and skip an oversized excerpt without discarding
later bounded candidates. The focused dispatcher and execution set then passed 66 tests.

The fresh automatic preparation subsequently selected:

- `scripts/provider_adapters.py` and `tests/test_provider_adapters.py` as expected paths;
- `AGENTS.md`, `adapters/providers.json`, and `scripts/provider_adapters.py` as 5,675 bytes of
  inline context;
- one exact quiet unittest verifier;
- one estimated model turn and a 4,096-byte largest result.

The first and only worker attempt used 21,591 input tokens in one model turn, issued zero tool
calls, changed no files, and returned `unknown`. Its reason was exact: the selected source did not
contain the provider-executable matching integration point invented by automatic preparation.
No verification ran, no worker was replayed, and the expected-path journal remained safe.

## Diagnosis

The worker protocol behaved correctly. The proof campaign did not: “select one useful code and
focused-test improvement” delegated product intent discovery to dispatch-time collateral
preparation. That phase can resolve exact implementation details for a clear campaign outcome; it
cannot reliably invent a useful product behavior and prove that its integration point exists from
generic focus-area terms.

The replacement proof must state a real semantic defect while still leaving exact paths, context,
verification, and budgets to automatic preparation. A locally confirmed candidate is cross-platform
provider instruction-path validation: `_safe_relative_path` currently accepts POSIX-relative input
but does not reject Windows-style backslash traversal or a root-like dot path.

## Boundary

This failure does not justify weakening the command-free worker, manually editing the persisted
capsule, or replaying Campaign 078. Publication, synchronization, Core upgrade, Windows execution,
and deployment remain excluded.
